value.summary:
Security review of the merged URL shortener at b7f1cdf. I read the code only and changed nothing. There are no critical or high findings. Most of the core controls hold up:
- **URL validation** (app/schemas/links.py:24-57) accepts only absolute http/https URLs with a host. It rejects javascript:, data: and file: URLs, plus control characters, whitespace and non-ASCII text. Because CR/LF can never get into a stored URL, the raw 307 Location header cannot be used for header injection.
- **No SSRF:** the service never fetches the target URLs.
- **SQL injection:** all queries go through the ORM with bound parameters only.
- **Log injection:** logs are written as escaped JSON and never include the client IP, headers or query strings.
- **Error handling:** errors map to generic {detail} bodies, so no stack traces or exception text reach clients.
- **Short codes** come from `secrets` (base62, 7 characters) and are backed by a unique constraint.
- **Client IP:** the raw IP never reaches the database. It becomes HMAC-SHA256(IP_HASH_SALT, ip) when a salt is set and NULL otherwise. This satisfies policy finding CMP-001 at redirect.py:48.

One change goes beyond the approved design (ADR-9 said no auth): the implementer added a router-level API key on /api/links*. It uses a constant-time comparison, requires a key of at least 16 characters, and rejects every request when no key is set. This closes design risk RK-1, but the documentation and API contract still say there is no authentication.

The remaining issues are defence-in-depth and privacy hardening:
- no rate limiting or body-size limits, especially on the public redirect, which writes to the database on every request;
- full referrer URLs (including query strings) and full user-agent strings are stored;
- click data is never deleted;
- keeping client IPs out of logs depends on an operator remembering uvicorn's --no-access-log flag;
- transitive dependencies are unpinned and test tools are mixed into the runtime requirements;
- /docs and /openapi.json are public;
- the salt is not held as a secret type in config;
- the docs are out of date about authentication.

The policy finding CHG-001 (requirements.txt changed) is consistent with the design pins; I saw no risky packages.
